<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The shared map location picker: a field to search for a place by name, an interactive map, the two
    ways of choosing a point on it by hand, and the control that changes how it is drawn.

    **What it is for.** Both Places forms — "Add a place" and the full place form — need exactly the
    same thing: a way to get to the place the user means, and a fixed pin that says which point will be
    saved. Rather than each screen growing its own copy, this file is the picker and both screens
    include it through LocationPickerView. That is also what makes the two behave identically, which
    matters most when editing: a place added one way and corrected the other should not feel like two
    different features.

    **The three routes, and why they are all here together.** They answer the same question for
    different users of the same screen, and none of them replaces the others:
      - **the search field** — fastest when the place has a name. "Patan Hospital" is three words
        against a great deal of panning.
      - **"I'M HERE"** — fastest when the user is standing in the place, which is the common case for
        a vet they have just visited.
      - **dragging the map** — the only route that works for a place with no name and no GPS fix: the
        back gate, the spot by the river, the stall on the corner.
    Whichever was used last is the location that gets saved; see PlaceLocationPicker.

    **Why the pin is in the middle of the map and never moves.** Dragging a small marker with a
    fingertip means covering the very thing being aimed at, and on a 40dp target most attempts miss.
    Every modern map app solves it the same way: hold the marker still in the centre and let the user
    move the world underneath it, so the target is the middle of the screen — the easiest place on a
    phone to aim at — and the finger is never over it. The coordinate saved is whatever the map's
    centre reports when it stops moving.

    The four controls over the map, arranged so that neither thumb rests on top of the pin:
      - **top-start, "I'M HERE"** — take the device's position and select it. Deliberately the loudest
        control here, because it is the one a first-time user needs and the only one whose purpose is
        not guessable from an icon.
      - **top-end, "MAP TYPE"** — normal or satellite. Opens a menu; changes nothing but the tiles.
      - **centre, the pin** — what is currently selected.
      - **bottom-end, the crosshair** — the same action as "I'm here", in the shape every map app has
        trained people to look for. One function behind both; see PlaceLocationPicker.useCurrentLocation.

    **Why the root is a vertical LinearLayout now, where it used to be the map's card.** The search
    field has to sit above the map and its results have to hang below the field, which is two things
    stacked — so the picker is a column whose last item is the map. The map keeps a fixed height of its
    own (the height the whole view used to be given), and the view as a whole is wrap_content: the
    screens that include it ask for no height at all any more.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:orientation="vertical">

    <!--
        "Search for a place" — the field, with a magnifier at the start and a clear button at the end.

        endIconMode=clear_text is TextInputLayout's own: it shows an ✕ only while there is text, and
        tapping it empties the field. That is worth having rather than writing, because clearing the
        box is how the user abandons a search, and the results list closes with it.

        imeOptions=actionSearch and inputType=text give the keyboard a search key instead of a
        newline, and singleLine keeps a long query on one line rather than growing the field and
        pushing the map down as it is typed.
    -->
    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
        android:id="@+id/searchLayout"
        style="@style/PetCare.AddPlace.Field"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        app:endIconContentDescription="@string/cd_place_search_clear"
        app:endIconMode="clear_text"
        app:startIconContentDescription="@null"
        app:startIconDrawable="@drawable/ic_search">

        <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
            android:id="@+id/searchInput"
            style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:hint="@string/place_search_hint"
            android:imeOptions="actionSearch"
            android:inputType="text"
            android:singleLine="true" />

    </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

    <!--
        The map, and the search results that hang over the top of it.

        **Why the results overlay the map rather than sitting between it and the field.** A list that
        pushed the map down would move every control on the screen each time a suggestion arrived —
        and suggestions arrive while the user is typing, so the map would jump about under the
        debounce. Laid over the map instead, the field stays where it is, the map stays where it is,
        and the panel appears and goes away like the dropdown it is.

        The frame's own height is the map's: a fixed height rather than a ratio, so the card is the
        same size whatever the screen. This is a control on a form, and one that grew with the display
        would push the fields below it off the bottom on a tall phone.
    -->
    <FrameLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/gps_map_height"
        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_sm">

        <!-- The map's card. It gives the rounded corners and the ground the map fades up out of, and
             the FrameLayout inside is what lets the controls be laid *over* the map by gravity alone
             rather than positioned against it. -->
        <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
            android:id="@+id/mapCard"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="match_parent"
            app:cardBackgroundColor="?attr/colorMapGround"
            app:cardElevation="@dimen/card_elevation"
            app:shapeAppearanceOverlay="@style/ShapeAppearance.PetCare.XLarge">

            <FrameLayout
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="match_parent">

                <!-- The map itself, filling the card exactly. Both dimensions are match_parent and
                     nothing is inset from it: the map must be the whole rectangle. See PlaceMapView. -->
                <com.prash.petcare.ui.place.PlaceMapView
                    android:id="@+id/map"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="match_parent"
                    android:contentDescription="@string/cd_map_picker" />

                <!-- Shown only when the map could not be loaded at all — offline, or the tile service
                     unreachable. It says so rather than leaving an empty card that looks like it is
                     still loading, and it names the way forward, because "I'm here" still works
                     without a map. -->
                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/mapUnavailableText"
                    style="@style/PetCare.AddPlace.MapPlaceholder"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_gravity="center"
                    android:paddingStart="@dimen/spacing_lg"
                    android:paddingEnd="@dimen/spacing_lg"
                    android:text="@string/map_picker_unavailable"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    tools:visibility="visible" />

                <!--
                    The fixed selection pin.

                    **Why it is translated upwards by half its height.** The coordinate saved is the
                    centre of the map — the middle of this FrameLayout — and a pin's *tip* is what
                    marks a point, not the middle of its balloon. Shifting the whole drawable up by
                    half its own height puts the tip exactly on the centre. See ic_map_pin.xml.

                    Hidden from TalkBack: it marks a point that the card below the map states in words.
                -->
                <ImageView
                    android:id="@+id/centrePin"
                    android:layout_width="@dimen/map_centre_pin_size"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/map_centre_pin_size"
                    android:layout_gravity="center"
                    android:importantForAccessibility="no"
                    android:src="@drawable/ic_map_pin"
                    android:translationY="@dimen/map_centre_pin_offset" />

                <!-- "I'M HERE". The crosshair icon rather than a pin, for the reason given in
                     ic_my_location.xml: a pin means "a saved place", which is what this produces, and
                     a crosshair means "where I am now", which is what it reads. -->
                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/imHereButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Map.ActionPill"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_gravity="top|start"
                    android:layout_margin="@dimen/gps_map_chip_inset"
                    android:text="@string/map_picker_im_here"
                    app:icon="@drawable/ic_my_location" />

                <!-- "MAP TYPE ▾". iconGravity textEnd puts the chevron after the label, which is what
                     says the pill opens a menu rather than performing an action. -->
                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/mapTypeButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Map.ActionPill"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_gravity="top|end"
                    android:layout_margin="@dimen/gps_map_chip_inset"
                    android:text="@string/map_type_button"
                    app:icon="@drawable/ic_chevron_down"
                    app:iconGravity="textEnd" />

                <!-- The familiar corner shortcut back to the user's own position. Icon only: it is the
                     one control on a map that needs no label anywhere in the world. The bottom-end
                     corner keeps it clear of the two pills and of the attribution credits the map
                     draws at bottom-start. -->
                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/recentreButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Map.RecentreButton"
                    android:layout_gravity="bottom|end"
                    android:layout_margin="@dimen/gps_map_chip_inset"
                    android:contentDescription="@string/cd_map_picker_recentre"
                    app:icon="@drawable/ic_my_location" />

            </FrameLayout>

        </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>

        <!--
            The suggestions panel: an elevated surface pinned to the top of the map, directly under the
            field, holding either the result rows or one line of status.

            Raised above the map's own elevation so it reads as floating over it rather than drawn into
            it — which is the difference between a dropdown and a label on the map.

            The bottom margin is what keeps a strip of map visible below a full list. Without it five
            rows would reach the foot of the frame and the panel would look like a page that had
            replaced the map, which is exactly the "don't open a new screen" this feature is built to
            avoid. The margin also bounds the list: the RecyclerView is wrap_content and scrolls only
            once its rows exceed what is left.

            Gone by default, and the view only shows it while the field has something to offer.
        -->
        <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
            android:id="@+id/searchResultsCard"
            style="@style/Widget.Material3.CardView.Elevated"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_gravity="top"
            android:layout_marginBottom="@dimen/place_search_results_bottom_inset"
            android:visibility="gone"
            app:cardBackgroundColor="?attr/colorSurface"
            app:cardElevation="@dimen/card_elevation_raised"
            app:shapeAppearanceOverlay="@style/ShapeAppearance.PetCare.Large"
            tools:visibility="visible">

            <!-- One of the two children below is shown at a time: the rows, or a single line saying
                 that the search is running or found nothing. They are siblings rather than one view
                 with swapped contents so that neither has to be emptied to show the other. -->
            <FrameLayout
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content">

                <androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView
                    android:id="@+id/searchResultsList"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:clipToPadding="false"
                    android:paddingTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                    android:paddingBottom="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                    tools:itemCount="3"
                    tools:listitem="@layout/item_place_search_result" />

                <!-- "Searching…", or "No places found". One line, because there is nothing to choose
                     from and so nothing to lay out — and it is said rather than left blank, since an
                     empty panel over the map looks like a rendering fault. -->
                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/searchStatusText"
                    style="@style/PetCare.PlaceSearch.Status"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:minHeight="@dimen/place_search_row_min_height"
                    android:paddingStart="@dimen/place_search_row_padding_horizontal"
                    android:paddingEnd="@dimen/place_search_row_padding_horizontal"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    tools:text="Searching…"
                    tools:visibility="visible" />

            </FrameLayout>

        </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>

    </FrameLayout>

</LinearLayout>
